  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x){e}^{-2x}+3).$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}\u226b\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x){e}^{-2x}+3) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)}{{e}^{2x}}+3) \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
